**Structured content** | **Semantic scale** | **Interactive fidelity**

{{ orgIntro(org) }}

Most design systems suffer from a quiet divergence between disciplines. A design team maintains a Figma kit filled with visual stickers, while engineers maintain production logic in code. Over time, those two universes drift apart into brittle catalogues of static pictures rather than interactive systems.

At UNDRR, Mangrove took a fundamentally different architectural path. As I have explored before, [Mangrove is primarily a content architecture masquerading as a design system](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/). I structured our content schemas directly into [Storybook](https://mangrove.undrr.org/): explicit props, content limits, semantic tokens, and brand overrides for five distinct digital properties (including UNDRR, PreventionWeb, and MCR2030).

Putting structure first gave me the leverage to automate what is usually manual toil. Because our components were defined by content contracts rather than arbitrary visuals, I used AI coding agents and custom plugins to translate Storybook's structured models into an interactive Figma design kit. Instead of drawing hundreds of ad-hoc shapes, I compiled a 297-variant library across 396 symbols that preserved component hierarchy, multi-brand themes, and real editorial presets.

## Why do conventional Figma kits fall out of sync?

**Static artboards cannot capture dynamic content contracts.** When a Figma component is treated as a visual drawing, it ignores the operational constraints that govern real websites where content overflows containers and editors toggle unexpected fields. Without an underlying content model, a UI kit is just a sticker sheet that quickly falls behind production code.

**Multi-brand scale makes manual drawing impossible.** Mangrove serves five distinct organizational brands, each requiring its own typography, palette, and spacing rules over shared component structures. Drawing five separate visual libraries by hand would have multiplied maintenance fivefold, guaranteeing drift. The Figma library had to reflect the same five-brand theming architecture that powered our CSS variable overrides in code.

**Interactivity requires semantic structure, not ad-hoc variants.** A card is not simply a box with arbitrary text; it carries specific content states. In Mangrove, components like the Book Card have strict editorial presets: `Default`, `NoImage`, `NoLink`, and `ButtonOnly`. When components are rooted in explicit content schemas, designers can toggle between supported configurations without breaking layout rules or inventing unsupported combinations.

