**AI coding agents acted as translators between schema and canvas.** Writing a Figma plugin from scratch to construct nested Auto Layout components, bind variables, and wire up variant properties is notoriously tedious. I directed coding agents to trace tokens across our repository, generate component construction recipes, and write the import plugin. Because the input was rigorous structured data rather than vague design prompts, the agents could reliably produce precise Figma node trees with correct padding, layout directions, and variable bindings.

**I separated component architecture from design-time edits.** Component structure, auto-layout constraints, and brand token modes belong strictly to the system. The specific copy, selected presets, and visibility toggles in an inserted instance belong to the designer. Enforcing this ownership boundary ensured that when the underlying library received an update, local editorial work was never wiped out.

## What did real-world application checks catch?

**A passing automated test masked an invisible control.** In automated headless runs, our disabled ComboBox component appeared technically sound, but in Figma, it was completely invisible. The conversion logic had passed an opacity value of `0.55` directly into a Figma variable field that expected a percentage integer of `55`. Inspecting the rendered node in Figma immediately caught what the simulated unit tests missed, allowing me to fix both the converter and the test harness.

**Inherited typography revealed browser rendering discrepancies.** A component's requested font weight in code does not guarantee which font face the operating system renders. Furthermore, small auxiliary help text inherited a fixed line-height from parent containers that a simple percentage conversion distorted in Figma. I compared browser measurements with Figma text boxes, calibrating line-height overrides to match actual DOM behavior.

**Real content boundaries collided with layout assumptions.** While stress-testing our Table component with authentic disaster risk data, longer cell labels collided with fixed divider lines. This made it clear that simply making text fields editable wasn't enough; the container needed flexible wrapping rules and documented editorial limits. I recorded that boundary directly in the component guidance for content practitioners and designers alike.

## What can the multi-brand pilot demonstrate?

**A production library now powers independent design files.** The pilot library generates 297 component variants across button modes, form controls, navigation, and content cards. To validate the kit in practice, I built a desktop and mobile resource-page example using 56 linked component instances. The test file incorporates customized headings, optional metadata blocks, and local designer notes, proving that product teams can compose complex pages without installing the importer plugin.

